==English==

===Etymology===
From English dialectal {{term|wanky|lang=en}}, alteration of {{etyl|enm}} {{term|wankel||unstable, shaky|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|wancol||unstable|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|wankō(j)ilaz|swaying, shaky, unstable|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Germanic|wankōjanan|to sway, be unsteady|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|wak-||wek-||wag-||weg-|to swing, be unsteady, slant, be crooked|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{term|wankle||wonky|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|wankel||shaky|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Wankelmut||fickleness, inconstancy, vacillation|lang=de}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|vanke||to wander|lang=da}} Not to be confused with "Wonka" (to make one think of chocolate).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|British}} {{IPA|/ˈwɔːŋ.kɪ/}}, {{SAMPA|/"wO:N.kI/}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|wŏngʹkē}}, {{IPA|/ˈwɑŋ.ki/|/ˈwɔŋ.ki/}}, {{SAMPA|/"wAN.ki/|/wON.ki/}}
* {{audio|en-us-wonky.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|ɒŋki}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|wonk|ier}}

# {{context|mostly|British|and|Australia}} [[lopsided|Lopsided]], [[misaligned]] or [[off-centre]].
# {{context|mostly|British|and|Australia}} [[feeble|Feeble]], [[shaky]] or [[rickety]].
# {{context|US|politics}} of or like a [[wonk]] (typically a [[policy wonk]])
# {{computing|especially [[Usenet]]}} Suffering from [[intermittent]] [[bug]]s; [[broken]].
# Generally [[incorrect]].

